  "question": {
    "text": "Consider a brass rod and a steel rod (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> longer than the brass rod) at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi>C.</mi></math> It is observed that on increasing temperatures of the two rods by same amount difference in lengths of the two rods does not change. Given that the thermal coefficient of linear expansion for steel and brass are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">℃</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>19</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">℃</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> respectively. The sum of lengths of the two rods at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
